WEBVTT

1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,850
A distributed denial-of-service event is an availability problem first.

2
00:00:04,850 --> 00:00:13,549
The immediate symptom is that a public service becomes slow, unstable, or unreachable because some part of its path is under pressure.

3
00:00:13,549 --> 00:00:24,558
That pressure might be network capacity at the edge, connection or state handling in an infrastructure component, or expensive behavior inside the application itself.

4
00:00:24,558 --> 00:00:28,792
In this course we keep those categories defensive and high level.

5
00:00:28,792 --> 00:00:32,334
The goal is not to study how hostile traffic is created.

6
00:00:32,334 --> 00:00:41,649
The goal is to understand what defenders need to keep real services operating when traffic is abnormal, intense, or intentionally disruptive.

7
00:00:41,649 --> 00:00:52,965
DDoS mitigation is the set of preparation and response capabilities that help a team absorb, filter, redirect, rate limit, cache, or gracefully degrade under that pressure.

8
00:00:52,965 --> 00:00:55,968
It is not one product and it is not one checkbox.

9
00:00:55,968 --> 00:01:05,822
A CDN may help for web traffic, a WAF may help with application requests, a DNS provider may protect name resolution, an Anycast edge may distribute load,

10
00:01:05,822 --> 00:01:12,366
and a scrubbing provider or upstream provider may filter large traffic before it reaches the origin.

11
00:01:12,366 --> 00:01:21,142
Those tools matter, but only when the architecture, routing, contacts, telemetry, and runbooks are ready before the incident begins.

12
00:01:21,142 --> 00:01:25,530
The preparation is often more important than the emergency button.

13
00:01:25,530 --> 00:01:34,229
Teams need an inventory of public services, dependencies, owners, provider contracts, emergency contacts, and change procedures.

14
00:01:34,229 --> 00:01:45,546
They need baselines that show what normal traffic looks like, dashboards that support fast classification, and escalation paths that do not depend on one person being awake.

15
00:01:45,546 --> 00:01:56,016
They also need clear assumptions: which services must remain available, which paths can degrade, and which emergency controls are acceptable for the business.

16
00:01:56,016 --> 00:01:59,326
During an event, a good runbook reduces confusion.

17
00:01:59,326 --> 00:02:12,952
It gives responders a sequence: confirm the signal, classify the pressure, protect critical paths, engage providers, apply scoped controls, communicate clearly, preserve evidence, and recover carefully.

18
00:02:12,952 --> 00:02:17,802
The better the preparation, the less the team has to invent under pressure.

19
00:02:17,802 --> 00:02:26,194
DDoS mitigation is availability engineering with security context, operational discipline, and calm communication built in.
